SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}A dense low-cost PM$_{2.5}$ network, gap-filled by machine learning, was carried all the way to district-level short-term mortality across Bihar in \textit{Nature Communications}: northern districts ran 1.2 times the mean exposure of southern ones, district mortality varied 1.7-fold and daily attributable deaths 3.2-fold.}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
IIT Kanpur (Tripathi group); network PM$_{2.5}$ fused with NFHS socioeconomic data and India-specific relative risks. State-level exposure inequity was limited; inter- and intra-regional inequity within Bihar was substantial.
\textbf{Where it is weak:} the abstract reports no node count, no calibration R$^2$ or RMSE and no uncertainty on attributable deaths. The mortality is a risk function applied to a sensor-derived surface, not an observed association, so any network calibration bias passes one-for-one into the burden.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} this is the clearest high-visibility case of low-cost sensing as the exposure layer of a burden estimate outside cities. Its value to the field rests on the calibration and propagated-uncertainty detail in the full text, which the weekly should pull."""

